// Week-off / working-day swap (org-wide or per shift) + attendance recalculation — schema draft
// STATUS: approved         (draft → approved → superseded)
// CLONE: workspace-1/hrms
// APPROVED_ON: 2026-10-26  (open questions decided below; Q4 = hybrid, the rest as recommended)
// Paste into https://dbdiagram.io (DBML). Plan (after approval): .plans/shift-day-swap-plan.md
//
// Header colours:
//   green  #2E7D32  NEW table
//   orange #EF6C00  existing table that GAINS columns
//   grey   #616161  existing, read only / referenced (columns trimmed)
// Column notes: [NEW] = added by this feature. "logical" = plain bigint, no FK constraint.

Project shift_day_swap {
  database_type: 'PostgreSQL'
  Note: '''
  ## Why
  1. An organisation runs alternate-Saturday week-offs (`shift_week_off_details`: day_of_week=6,
     week_of_month in {2,4}). HR now needs to swap specific dates for the WHOLE organisation (or
     chosen shifts): a normally-working Saturday becomes a week-off, and a normally-off Saturday
     becomes a working day. Past-dated swaps must also re-settle attendance already computed.
  2. Today a day's type is derived ONLY from rules (`calendar_resolver_service.resolve_day_type`:
     Holiday > weekly rule > Working). There is no per-date exception, so this cannot be expressed.
     Editing the weekly rule (`PUT /shifts/{id}/week-offs`) is wrong for it: it is whole-set,
     recurring, not dated, and it locks all computed history for the shift.
  3. This adds ONE table, `shift_day_overrides`: "on this date, for this shift (or every shift),
     the day is WeekOff / Working". The resolver reads it between the holiday check and the weekly
     rule. Everything else (attendance rows, audit, OT, payroll summary) is existing tables that
     the recalculation rewrites through the existing `recompute_attendance_day(force=True)`.

  ## Resolution order (after this change)
  Holiday (calendar)  >  override for (shift, date)  >  override for (all shifts, date)  >  weekly rule  >  Working.
  A shift-specific row beats an org-wide row (shift_master_id NULL) for the same date.

  ## Feasibility
  - ✅ Tenant scoping: `care_delivery_program_master_id` NOT NULL. No RLS — every read must
       filter on it: the resolver lookup (cdp + date range), the list/preview endpoints, the
       recalc job. The resolver's `is_shift_week_off(db, shift_master_id, date)` has no tenant
       argument today, so it must read the shift's CDP (or take it as a param) before matching
       the org-wide (NULL shift) rows — otherwise another tenant's org-wide row could match.
  - ✅ Conventions: `id bigint`, NOT NULL booleans/varchars with server defaults, timestamptz.
       Reserved names: none. `day_type` is varchar(10), same vocabulary as
       `employee_attendance_details.day_type` ('WeekOff' | 'Working'; 'Holiday' is never stored here).
  - ✅ Soft delete: is_active / archive / archived_by_id / archived_at. Removing a swap archives the
       row and sets recalc_status back to 'Pending' so the days are re-settled to the rule.
       The resolver and every list query must exclude archive=true / is_active=false. The
       recalc job must still pick up archived rows whose recalc_status is Pending.
  - ✅ Audit: created_by_id / updated_by_id / created_at / updated_at. Per-attendance-day
       old→new is NOT a column here: it goes to the existing append-only `attendance_audit_logs`
       (action 'DayTypeOverridden', old_value/new_value JSONB, reason = this row's reason). No
       schema change there: `action` is varchar(40) and the value list is only a model comment.
  - ✅ FKs: real FKs to care_delivery_program_masters and shift_masters. No polymorphism.
  - ✅ Indexes: resolver path (cdp, override_date); uniqueness per scope via TWO partial unique
       indexes (a NULL shift_master_id defeats a plain composite unique); recalc-queue lookup.
  - ✅ Existing data: brand-new table, nothing to backfill, no existing column touched.
  - ✅ Overlap: closest are `shift_week_off_details` (recurring rule, no date, whole-set replace)
       and `holiday_details` (Public holiday => day_type Holiday, pays/reports differently, cannot
       turn a week-off INTO a working day). Neither can hold a dated WeekOff<->Working exception.
  - ✅ Write paths: (a) new HR endpoints (create/bulk-flip/list/remove), (b) the recalc runner.
       No nhforms submit, CSV import or employee-app path writes it.
  - ⚠️ Migrations: plain Alembic revision on the HRMS branch, applied via
       `scripts/migrate.py upgrade heads`. The shared hrms-dev DB cannot be `alembic upgrade`d
       (stamped at orphan 8012104013b3) — the DDL there must be a hand-written, existence-guarded
       migration, like c4f1a9d2e7b8. No nhforms-owned table is touched.
  - ✅ RBAC: gate writes on `require_grid_permission(..., "UPDATE", "ATTENDANCE")` via
       `_require_shift_admin` (exactly what `replace_week_offs` uses — shifts have no grid cell
       of their own); reads on READ ATTENDANCE. Add cases to test_authz.py.
  - ✅ Cloud fit: no local disk or in-process state. Recalc progress lives in recalc_status, not
       in memory, so any Fargate replica can resume it; guarded by the existing per-CDP advisory
       lock pattern in `attendance_assembler_job`.
  - ⚠️ Other readers of "is this a week-off?" that must switch to the override-aware rule or the
       swap will contradict itself: `week_off_from_rows` (the one shared matcher) and its callers
       `is_shift_week_off`, `resolve_day_types_for_pairs` (attendance register), the shift roster
       grid, shift auto-assign `skip_week_offs`, and leave day-counting (`leave/service.py`
       `resolve_day_type`, exclude_week_offs). `payroll/service.py` calls resolve_day_type with
       NO shift (Sat/Sun fallback) — see Q1.
  - ⚠️ Side effects of a recalculated PAST day (not schema, but they decide whether this ships):
       * Working -> WeekOff where people punched: row stays 'Present' (WeekOff + worked>0) but
         `overtime_minutes` becomes the whole day, so `overtime/sync.sync_day` creates Pending OT.
       * WeekOff -> Working where nobody punched: closed day becomes 'Absent'.
       * Approved leave over a date that was a week-off has no `leave_request_day_details` row
         for it; the new Working day will not see that leave. Ledger is NOT recomputed (Q5).
       * Payroll already run over the dates is not re-run; Processed OT rows are never touched.

  ## Alternatives considered
  - Effective-date the weekly rule (add effctv_dt/trmntn_dt to shift_week_off_details): handles
    "from Nov the off Saturdays are 1st/3rd/5th" but not a one-off swap, and still needs a
    rewrite of the whole-set replace API. Rejected for v1 (Q9).
  - Model the swapped-OFF Saturday as a Public holiday: wrong day_type, wrong leave/pay rules,
    and cannot express "off day becomes working".
  - One row per (shift, date) only, no NULL = all shifts: would not cover shifts created after
    the swap, and "entire organisation" would write shifts x dates rows. Rejected.
  - Separate header table for a swap batch: not needed — reason and recalc state sit on each row
    and a "swap batch" is just N rows created in one request.

  ## Open questions
  1. Org-wide scope: does a NULL-shift row also apply to employees with NO shift assignment
     (the Sat/Sun fallback used by payroll `working_days` and leave)? Recommend yes.
  2. Precedence: a swap that lands on a Public holiday — holiday still wins? Recommend yes.
  3. Past / locked / paid dates: allow a swap on dates whose attendance is is_locked (forced
     recompute, rows stay locked, audited)? And refuse when the date is inside a payroll_periods
     row that is processed/approved/locked? Recommend: allow locked, refuse paid periods with a
     clear message ("reopen payroll first").
  4. Recalculation: synchronous for small orgs only, or always queued (Pending -> Running ->
     Done/Failed picked up by a per-CDP job + an on-demand "Recalculate" button)? Recommend
     always queued; a 5,000-employee org x 2 dates is ~10,000 recomputes.
  5. Existing approved leave over swapped days: report only (list overlaps in the preview),
     no leave-ledger changes in v1? Recommend report only.
  6. Working -> WeekOff on a past date with punches creates a day of Pending OT. Acceptable as is
     (HR can reject), or suppress OT for override-created week-offs? Recommend acceptable + preview count.
  7. "Working" override with half-day thresholds? Out of scope v1 (full day only).
  8. Bulk "flip every Saturday in a date range" creates a row for EVERY Saturday including a 5th
     one (which the 2/4 rule treats as working, so it flips to off). Confirm that is intended.
  9. Is a permanent recurring pattern change (not dated exceptions) also needed? If yes, that is
     the effective-dated-rule alternative above, a separate schema draft.

  ## Decided (2026-10-26)
  1. Yes. An org-wide row (shift_master_id NULL) also applies to employees with no shift
     assignment and to payroll's no-shift `resolve_day_type` calls.
  2. Holiday still wins over any override.
  3. Locked dates are allowed (forced recompute, row stays locked, audited). A date inside a
     closed payroll period is refused with a clear message.
  4. HYBRID recalculation. A small swap is recalculated inline in the request; a large one is saved
     as recalc_status 'Pending' and settled by a per-CDP scheduled job. Both run the same function.
     The size threshold is a Settings value, counted in employee-days (see the plan).
  5. Existing approved leave over swapped days is reported in the preview only. No ledger changes.
  6. OT created by a Working -> WeekOff past day is accepted; the preview shows the count.
  7. Out of scope: half-day "Working" override.
  8. A bulk flip covers every matching weekday in the range, a 5th Saturday included.
  9. Out of scope: a permanent recurring-pattern change (no effective-dating of
     shift_week_off_details). A separate DBML if ever needed.
  '''
}

// varchar(10): 'WeekOff' | 'Working'. 'Holiday' is never an override value — it comes from the calendar.
Enum shift_day_override_day_type {
  WeekOff
  Working
}

// varchar(10): Pending -> Running -> Done | Failed.
Enum shift_day_override_recalc_status {
  Pending
  Running
  Done
  Failed
}

Table shift_day_overrides [headercolor: #2E7D32] {
  id bigint [pk, increment]
  care_delivery_program_master_id bigint [not null, ref: > care_delivery_program_masters.id, note: 'tenant']
  shift_master_id bigint [ref: > shift_masters.id, note: 'NULL = every shift in this CDP (org-wide). Set = that shift only; beats the org-wide row for the same date.']
  override_date date [not null, note: 'the calendar date at the work location (not UTC)']
  day_type varchar(10) [not null, note: "'WeekOff' | 'Working'"]
  reason varchar(255) [not null, note: 'HR must say why; copied to attendance_audit_logs.reason on every day it changes']

  recalc_status varchar(10) [not null, default: 'Pending', note: "'Pending'|'Running'|'Done'|'Failed'. Set back to 'Pending' whenever the row is created, edited or archived."]
  recalculated_at timestamptz [note: 'when the last recalculation finished']
  recalc_result jsonb [note: 'never queried: {employees, days_recomputed, days_changed, days_force_locked, ot_rows_created, failed: [..]}']

  is_active boolean [not null, default: true]
  archive boolean [not null, default: false]
  archived_by_id bigint [note: 'logical']
  archived_at timestamptz
  created_by_id bigint [note: 'logical']
  updated_by_id bigint [note: 'logical']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  Indexes {
    (care_delivery_program_master_id, override_date) [name: 'ix_shift_day_overrides_cdp_date', note: 'resolver path: all overrides for a tenant over a date range']
    (care_delivery_program_master_id, override_date) [unique, name: 'uq_shift_day_overrides_org_wide', note: 'WHERE shift_master_id IS NULL AND archive = false']
    (care_delivery_program_master_id, shift_master_id, override_date) [unique, name: 'uq_shift_day_overrides_shift', note: 'WHERE shift_master_id IS NOT NULL AND archive = false']
    (care_delivery_program_master_id, recalc_status) [name: 'ix_shift_day_overrides_recalc_queue', note: "WHERE recalc_status IN ('Pending','Running','Failed'); includes archived rows (their undo still has to be recalculated)"]
  }

  Note: '[NEW] app/models/shift_day_override.py -> ShiftDayOverride (table shift_day_overrides)'
}

Table care_delivery_program_masters [headercolor: #616161] {
  id bigint [pk]
}

Table shift_masters [headercolor: #616161] {
  id bigint [pk]
  care_delivery_program_master_id bigint
  name varchar(120)
  start_time time
  end_time time
  policy_config jsonb [note: 'regularization_window_days decides when a recomputed day auto-locks']
}

Table shift_week_off_details [headercolor: #616161] {
  id bigint [pk]
  care_delivery_program_master_id bigint
  shift_master_id bigint [ref: > shift_masters.id]
  day_of_week int [note: 'ISO 1=Mon..7=Sun']
  week_of_month int [note: 'NULL = every week; 2 and 4 with day_of_week=6 is the alternate-Saturday rule']
  is_half_day boolean
  Note: 'Read only. The recurring rule an override sits on top of. NOT changed by this feature (no lock, no replace).'
}

Table employee_attendance_details [headercolor: #616161] {
  id bigint [pk]
  care_delivery_program_master_id bigint
  employee_master_id bigint
  attendance_date date
  shift_master_id bigint
  day_type varchar(10) [note: "'Working'|'WeekOff'|'Holiday' - rewritten by the recalculation"]
  attendance_status varchar(30) [note: 'rewritten by the recalculation']
  worked_minutes int
  overtime_minutes int
  payable_day_fraction numeric
  is_locked boolean [note: 'recompute(force=True) re-resolves a locked row and leaves it locked']
  Note: 'Read/rewritten through recompute_attendance_day only. No new column. Days with no row yet resolve live under the new rule, no recompute needed.'
}

Table attendance_audit_logs [headercolor: #616161] {
  id bigint [pk]
  care_delivery_program_master_id bigint
  employee_master_id bigint
  action varchar(40) [note: "gains the VALUE 'DayTypeOverridden' (comment only, no DDL)"]
  old_value jsonb [note: "{day_type, attendance_status, worked_minutes, overtime_minutes, shift_day_override_id}"]
  new_value jsonb
  performed_by_id bigint
  performed_by_role varchar(20)
  reason varchar(500)
  Note: 'Append-only. One row per attendance day whose day_type/status actually changed.'
}

Table payroll_periods [headercolor: #616161] {
  id bigint [pk]
  care_delivery_program_master_id bigint
  period_start date
  period_end date
  status_master_id bigint
  processed_at timestamptz
  approved_at timestamptz
  locked_at timestamptz
  Note: 'Read only, for the guard in Q3: refuse a swap whose date falls in a processed/approved/locked period.'
}

TableGroup shift_day_swap {
  shift_day_overrides
  shift_masters
  shift_week_off_details
  employee_attendance_details
  attendance_audit_logs
  payroll_periods
}
